# .test-honesty-baseline — tests and checks that currently assert less
# than they appear to. Generated by scripts/ci/lint-test-honesty.sh.
#
#   H1-BLIND-NEGATION    `! <cmd>` proving only 'exited non-zero', with
#                        no assertion on WHY. Fix: assert the error text.
#   H2-SWALLOWED-VERDICT a verdict function replaces a command failure
#                        with a literal (`|| echo "[]"`) and measures it.
#   H3-SKIP-STATEMENTS   per-file count of `skip` statements. bats scores
#                        a skip as ok. SHRINK-ONLY: the count may go DOWN
#                        (lower the number in the same MR) but never up.
#   H4-HOST-BLIND        a host-dependent branch with no NWP_* knob to
#                        simulate the absent tool, that does not fail closed.
#
# SHRINK-ONLY. Deleting a row is a fix. Adding one is a recorded decision:
# say in the commit message why the check is going in blind.
# Regenerate: scripts/ci/lint-test-honesty.sh --update-baseline
# Inspect:    scripts/ci/lint-test-honesty.sh --list
#=
#= RECORDED DECISIONS (why a row is here rather than fixed) — 2026-08-03, MR !317
#=
#= H3 tests/unit/test-ssd-restore-catalogue-content.bats 1
#= H3 tests/unit/test-ssd-seed-courses.bats             1
#= H3 tests/unit/test-auth-logic.bats                   1
#=   All three are the SAME deliberate shape and it is the one H4 asks for: a
#=   `require_php` helper that FAILS when php is absent, and skips only when the
#=   operator has set NWP_ALLOW_MISSING_PHP=1 on purpose. The skip statement
#=   exists so that the absent-tool path can be exercised deliberately; it
#=   cannot fire by accident. Counting it is correct — this rule counts skips
#=   that EXIST, not skips that fire — and recording it is the honest answer.
#=
#= H3 tests/unit/test-ci-lint-commands.bats 2
#=   Neither line can skip anything: one `printf`s a skip into a synthetic
#=   fixture, the other `grep`s the repo for the skip idiom. The rule counts
#=   executable `skip` in command position and cannot tell a fixture writer
#=   from a skip. Left as a known over-count rather than teaching the counter
#=   another exception it would then be trusted to apply correctly.
#=
